* Better UI
  - Mapping by pressing buttons ("press the Xbox button to change, then pick the Switch button") instead of typing names
* Running on MacOS & Linux
  - Only XInput, the Windows Bluetooth COM-port detection and the installer are Windows-specific; protocol and links are portable
* Support more controllers -- currently assumes XBox controller inputs (XInput)
  - Read controllers through SDL (PlayStation, Switch Pro, generic); pairs with the MacOS & Linux item
  - Confirm and document the Xbox Adaptive Controller (XInput, so it most likely already works)
* Support for other consoles
  - Switch 1: should already work (same HORIPAD), untested
  - PlayStation / Xbox: not possible -- they require authentication chips in controllers
* CLAUDE.md file

Ease of use
* Start with Windows, or start automatically when the controller connects
* Code signing (removes the "Windows protected your PC" warning and most antivirus false alarms)

Accessibility
* One button sending a combination of buttons
* Stick and trigger options: swap sticks, d-pad <-> stick, deadzones, sensitivity, inverted axes, trigger threshold
* Two-controller co-pilot: merge two controllers into one on the Switch (assisted play)
* Status that doesn't rely on one sense: notifications/tray state alongside beeps and the LED; screen-reader-friendly console progress
* Per-game mapping profiles with a quick switch

Broad usage
* Several Picos on one network: unique Wi-Fi/Bluetooth names (e.g. from the chip ID) and choosing which Pico to use
* Pro Controller mode: motion controls, rumble, Capture -- biggest compatibility gap, and the biggest piece of work
* Pico 2 W (RP2350) firmware build
* Handheld mode over USB-C: test and document
* Translated prompts and docs
